{"status": 200, "url_final": "https://metahub.ai/", "html": "<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n  <head>\n    <meta charset=\"utf-8\" />\n    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\" />\n    <title>metahub \u00b7 the open ecosystem for AI tools</title>\n    <meta\n      name=\"description\"\n      content=\"metahub is building open infrastructure for AI tools: a registry that installs anywhere, an open-source eval framework, and an ecosystem built in public.\"\n    />\n    <link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://metahub.ai/\" />\n    <meta name=\"robots\" content=\"index, follow, max-image-preview:large\" />\n    <meta name=\"author\" content=\"metahub\" />\n    <meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-08-20\" />\n    <meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"metahub \u00b7 the open ecosystem for AI tools\" />\n    <meta\n      property=\"og:description\"\n      content=\"The metahub registry, and Assay: our eval framework, now open source.\"\n    />\n    <meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"website\" />\n    <meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://metahub.ai/\" />\n    <meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"metahub\" />\n    <!-- Own card, not registry.metahub.ai/opengraph-image: that one is\n         generated per-catalog, renders live artifact counts and says \"find,\n         install, and publish tools for your editor\" \u2014 accurate for the\n         registry, wrong for the umbrella site, and it made every share of\n         metahub.ai look like a share of the registry. Regenerate from\n         /og-card.html; see the comment in that file. -->\n    <meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://metahub.ai/og.png\" />\n    <meta property=\"og:image:type\" content=\"image/png\" />\n    <meta property=\"og:image:width\" content=\"1512\" />\n    <meta property=\"og:image:height\" content=\"794\" />\n    <meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"metahub \u00b7 the open ecosystem for AI tools\" />\n    <meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\" />\n    <meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"metahub \u00b7 the open ecosystem for AI tools\" />\n    <meta\n      name=\"twitter:description\"\n      content=\"The metahub registry, and Assay: our eval framework, now open source.\"\n    />\n    <meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://metahub.ai/og.png\" />\n    <meta name=\"twitter:image:alt\" content=\"metahub \u00b7 the open ecosystem for AI tools\" />\n    <script type=\"application/ld+json\">\n      {\n        \"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n        \"@graph\": [\n          {\n            \"@type\": \"Organization\",\n            \"@id\": \"https://metahub.ai/#organization\",\n            \"name\": \"metahub\",\n            \"description\": \"metahub is building open infrastructure for AI tools: a registry that installs anywhere, an open-source eval framework, and an ecosystem built in public.\",\n            \"contactPoint\": {\n              \"@type\": \"ContactPoint\",\n              \"contactType\": \"technical support\",\n              \"email\": \"support@metahub.ai\",\n              \"url\": \"https://github.com/metahub-ai/metahub-docs/issues\",\n              \"availableLanguage\": [\"en\"]\n            },\n            \"url\": \"https://metahub.ai\",\n            \"logo\": \"https://registry.metahub.ai/icon\",\n            \"address\": {\n              \"@type\": \"PostalAddress\",\n              \"streetAddress\": \"3020 Bernal Avenue, Ste 110, #2077\",\n              \"addressLocality\": \"Pleasanton\",\n              \"addressRegion\": \"CA\",\n              \"postalCode\": \"94566\",\n              \"addressCountry\": \"US\"\n            },\n            \"sameAs\": [\n              \"https://github.com/metahub-ai\",\n              \"https://github.com/metahub-ai/assay\",\n              \"https://github.com/metahub-ai/metahub-docs\",\n              \"https://registry.metahub.ai\",\n              \"https://developer.metahub.ai\"\n            ]\n          },\n          {\n            \"@type\": \"WebSite\",\n            \"@id\": \"https://metahub.ai/#website\",\n            \"name\": \"metahub\",\n            \"url\": \"https://metahub.ai\",\n            \"inLanguage\": \"en\",\n            \"publisher\": {\n              \"@type\": \"Organization\",\n              \"@id\": \"https://metahub.ai/#organization\",\n              \"name\": \"metahub\",\n              \"logo\": \"https://registry.metahub.ai/icon\"\n            }\n          },\n          {\n            \"@type\": \"FAQPage\",\n            \"@id\": \"https://metahub.ai/#faq\",\n            \"url\": \"https://metahub.ai/\",\n            \"name\": \"metahub \u2014 common questions\",\n            \"isPartOf\": { \"@id\": \"https://metahub.ai/#website\" },\n            \"about\": { \"@id\": \"https://metahub.ai/#organization\" },\n            \"mainEntity\": [\n              {\n                \"@type\": \"Question\",\n                \"name\": \"How do I install metahub?\",\n                \"acceptedAnswer\": {\n                  \"@type\": \"Answer\",\n                  \"text\": \"One command, and a plain shell script you can read before you run it: curl -fsSL https://metahub.ai/install.sh | sh. It installs the mh CLI and the metahub MCP server through your package manager, adds them to PATH, and connects the MCP server to the AI editors it finds on your machine, a step you can skip and reverse later. After that a single mh install command works in any supported editor.\"\n                }\n              },\n              {\n                \"@type\": \"Question\",\n                \"name\": \"Which AI clients does metahub work with?\",\n                \"acceptedAnswer\": {\n                  \"@type\": \"Answer\",\n                  \"text\": \"Eleven, including Claude Code, Cursor, and Zed. The same install command wires an artifact into whichever of them you use, so a skill, MCP server, agent, or plugin does not need separate setup instructions per editor.\"\n                }\n              },\n              {\n                \"@type\": \"Question\",\n                \"name\": \"What does metahub send back when I install a tool?\",\n                \"acceptedAnswer\": {\n                  \"@type\": \"Answer\",\n                  \"text\": \"Anonymous, opt-out usage statistics, and nothing else. Installed tools report invocation counts, latency, and error rates back to the person who published them. Never your prompts, never your files. You can switch the telemetry off.\"\n                }\n              },\n              {\n                \"@type\": \"Question\",\n                \"name\": \"How does metahub decide what gets listed?\",\n                \"acceptedAnswer\": {\n                  \"@type\": \"Answer\",\n                  \"text\": \"Every artifact is evaluated before it reaches the catalog. The evaluation runs on Assay, our open-source framework released under Apache-2.0, so both the checks and the scoring are public. You can run the same evaluation on your own repository, and open a pull request when you think a check is wrong.\"\n                }\n              }\n            ]\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    </script>\n    <!-- Real files, not a data: URI. The favicon was declared only as an\n         inline data: URI, which Google\u2019s favicon crawler cannot fetch \u2014 it\n         needs a crawlable URL. With no fetchable icon, and /favicon.ico\n         404ing as well, Google had nothing to show beside metahub.ai in search\n         results and substituted an unrelated mark. Browsers resolve a data:\n         URI fine, which is why this only ever surfaced in the SERP.\n\n         Keep these as real paths. Regenerate from icon.svg if the mark\n         changes; scripts/ has no generator, they were rasterised once. -->\n    <link rel=\"icon\" type=\"image/svg+xml\" href=\"/icon.svg\" />\n    <link rel=\"alternate icon\" href=\"/favicon.ico\" sizes=\"16x16 32x32 48x48\" />\n    <link rel=\"apple-touch-icon\" href=\"/icon-180.png\" />\n    <meta name=\"theme-color\" content=\"#f4eee3\" />\n    <link\n      rel=\"preload\"\n      href=\"/fonts/archivo-normal.woff2\"\n      as=\"font\"\n      type=\"font/woff2\"\n      crossorigin\n    />\n    <link\n      rel=\"preload\"\n      href=\"/fonts/martian-mono-normal.woff2\"\n      as=\"font\"\n      type=\"font/woff2\"\n      crossorigin\n    />\n    <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"/styles.css\" />\n    <script src=\"/page.js\" defer></script>\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <header class=\"masthead\">\n      <div class=\"wrap\">\n        <a class=\"wordmark\" href=\"#top\" aria-label=\"metahub\">\n          <svg width=\"22\" height=\"22\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\n            <circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"2.6\" fill=\"#b0492c\" />\n            <g stroke=\"#1d1510\" stroke-width=\"1.7\" stroke-linecap=\"round\">\n              <line x1=\"12\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"12\" y2=\"3\" />\n              <line x1=\"12\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"20\" y2=\"7\" />\n              <line x1=\"12\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"21\" y2=\"14\" />\n              <line x1=\"12\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"17\" y2=\"21\" />\n              <line x1=\"12\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"7\" y2=\"21\" />\n              <line x1=\"12\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"3\" y2=\"15\" />\n              <line x1=\"12\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"4\" y2=\"6\" />\n            </g>\n          </svg>\n          metahub\n        </a>\n        <nav class=\"mast-links\" aria-label=\"Primary\">\n          <a class=\"hide-sm\" href=\"https://registry.metahub.ai\">/registry</a>\n          <a class=\"hide-sm\" href=\"https://developer.metahub.ai\">/developer</a>\n          <a class=\"hide-sm\" href=\"#evals\">/eval</a>\n          <!-- Same-site destination, so no slash prefix on the label (the\n               prefix marks a jump to another metahub site). This was the only\n               place a reader could not reach the FAQ from without scrolling to\n               the footer, and it left the landing page with almost no\n               same-origin outbound links for a crawler to follow. -->\n          <a class=\"hide-sm\" href=\"/faq\">faq</a>\n          <a class=\"hide-sm\" href=\"/about\">about</a>\n          <a class=\"hide-sm\" href=\"/contact\">contact</a>\n          <!-- No CTA button here. It read \"Browse the registry\" and pointed at\n               registry.metahub.ai/browse, sitting directly beside /registry\n               pointing at registry.metahub.ai \u2014 two adjacent links to the same\n               destination, which is the duplication that made the masthead feel\n               busy. The registry section carries \"Open the registry\" and\n               \"Publish to registry\" where a visitor has just been told what\n               they are. -->\n          <!-- Below 640px the three nav links were simply display:none, so the\n               phone masthead offered one button and no way to reach /registry,\n               /developer or the evals section at all. This is the same\n               hamburger-into-a-list pattern the registry uses, so the two\n               surfaces behave alike. -->\n          <button\n            type=\"button\"\n            class=\"mast-toggle\"\n            id=\"menubtn\"\n            aria-label=\"Open menu\"\n            aria-expanded=\"false\"\n            aria-controls=\"mobilemenu\"\n          >\n            <svg width=\"20\" height=\"20\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\n              <path\n                d=\"M4 7h16M4 12h16M4 17h16\"\n                stroke=\"currentColor\"\n                stroke-width=\"2\"\n                stroke-linecap=\"round\"\n              />\n            </svg>\n          </button>\n        </nav>\n      </div>\n      <!-- Ordered the way the page itself is: the two destinations that leave\n           this site, then the on-page anchor, then the primary action. -->\n      <div class=\"mobile-menu\" id=\"mobilemenu\" hidden>\n        <a href=\"https://registry.metahub.ai\">/registry</a>\n        <a href=\"https://developer.metahub.ai\">/developer</a>\n        <a href=\"#evals\">/eval</a>\n        <a href=\"/faq\">faq</a>\n        <a href=\"/about\">about</a>\n        <a href=\"/contact\">contact</a>\n      </div>\n    </header>\n\n    <main id=\"top\">\n      <!-- \u2500\u2500 hero \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500 -->\n      <div class=\"hero\">\n        <div class=\"wrap\">\n          <h1 class=\"display rv\" style=\"--d: 90ms\">\n            AI tools are exploding.<br /><span class=\"turn\">The infrastructure isn't.</span>\n          </h1>\n          <p class=\"stand rv\" style=\"--d: 190ms\">\n            Thousands of skills, MCP servers, agents, and plugins, scattered across GitHub with no\n            install story, no quality bar, and no way to tell what actually works.\n            <strong>metahub is building the missing infrastructure, in the open.</strong> We're\n            starting with two core pieces: a universal registry that installs anywhere, and the\n            open-source eval framework that powers it.\n          </p>\n          <!-- The two buttons are the two pieces the paragraph above names,\n               each pointing at the section that explains it. Previously the\n               first went straight to registry.metahub.ai, which duplicated\n               the masthead CTA and left #registry with no inbound link at\n               all. The masthead is sticky, so the direct route to the\n               catalog is on screen the whole way down regardless. -->\n          <div class=\"hero-actions rv\" style=\"--d: 280ms\">\n            <a class=\"btn\" href=\"#registry\">The registry</a>\n            <a class=\"btn ghost\" href=\"#evals\">The eval framework</a>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"cmd rv\" id=\"cmd\" style=\"--d: 370ms\">\n            <span class=\"p\">$</span>\n            <span id=\"cmdtext\">curl -fsSL https://metahub.ai/install.sh | sh</span>\n            <button type=\"button\" id=\"copybtn\" aria-label=\"Copy install command\">Copy</button>\n          </div>\n          <p class=\"cmd-note rv\" style=\"--d: 450ms\">\n            One command. Then\n            <span style=\"font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 13px\"\n              >mh install &lt;anything&gt;</span\n            >\n            works in any harness: Claude Code, Cursor, Zed, and 8 more.\n          </p>\n          <p class=\"cmd-note rv\" style=\"--d: 520ms\">\n            What it does: a plain shell script you can\n            <a href=\"https://metahub.ai/install.sh\">read before running</a>: installs the mh CLI and\n            MCP server via your package manager, adds them to PATH, and connects the MCP server to\n            the editors it detects (skippable, reversible). Installed tools report anonymous,\n            opt-out usage stats to their publisher. Never your prompts, never your files.\n            <a href=\"https://registry.metahub.ai/docs\">Full details.</a>\n          </p>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n\n      <!-- \u2500\u2500 01 registry \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500 -->\n      <section class=\"sec-registry\" id=\"registry\">\n        <div class=\"wrap\">\n          <div class=\"sec-head rv\">\n            <span class=\"sec-num\">01</span>\n            <h2 class=\"display\">The registry</h2>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"reg-grid\">\n            <div>\n              <p class=\"lede rv\">\n                A registry for AI tools. Every artifact is pinned to an exact commit, scanned and\n                evaluated before it goes public, and installs with one command into\n                <strong>any harness, 12 and counting</strong>. Browse by use case, check the eval\n                report, read real reviews, install. <strong>Free.</strong>\n              </p>\n              <!-- The numbers in the markup are a fallback, not the source of\n                   truth: they render immediately, survive a failed fetch and a\n                   JS-off visit, and are replaced with live values on load (see\n                   the counts fetch at the bottom of the page). Before this they\n                   were the only value and drifted the moment the catalog moved.\n\n                   `data-count` names the field each cell reads. vendor-official\n                   reads `official`, which the counts endpoint now returns. It used to be\n                   hardcoded, on the reasoning that official-ness is an owner allowlist\n                   resolved per artifact and so had no single number to read \u2014 true of\n                   the consumer, but the portal can count it, and the figure went stale\n                   the moment the allowlist grew. -->\n              <div class=\"figures rv\" aria-label=\"Catalog numbers\">\n                <div>\n                  <span class=\"num\" data-count=\"total\">3,348</span\n                  ><span class=\"lbl\">artifacts live</span>\n                </div>\n                <div>\n                  <span class=\"num\" data-count=\"skill\">2,286</span><span class=\"lbl\">skills</span>\n                </div>\n                <div>\n                  <span class=\"num\" data-count=\"mcp\">429</span><span class=\"lbl\">MCP servers</span>\n                </div>\n                <div>\n                  <span class=\"num\" data-count=\"plugin\">631</span><span class=\"lbl\">plugins</span>\n                </div>\n                <div>\n                  <span class=\"num\" data-count=\"categories\">14</span\n                  ><span class=\"lbl\">use-case categories</span>\n                </div>\n                <div>\n                  <span class=\"num\" data-count=\"official\">327</span\n                  ><span class=\"lbl\">vendor-official</span>\n                </div>\n              </div>\n              <ul class=\"reg-steps rv\">\n                <li>\n                  <span class=\"n\">01</span\n                  ><span\n                    >Find a tool at <code>registry.metahub.ai</code>, by category, search, or the\n                    official shelf.</span\n                  >\n                </li>\n                <li>\n                  <span class=\"n\">02</span\n                  ><span\n                    >Run <code>mh install &lt;slug&gt;</code>. Commit-pinned: you get exactly what\n                    was evaluated.</span\n                  >\n                </li>\n                <li>\n                  <span class=\"n\">03</span\n                  ><span>It works across your editors. Review it so the next person knows.</span>\n                </li>\n              </ul>\n              <!-- Two doors out of this section, because it describes two\n                   audiences: people installing artifacts, and people shipping\n                   them. Publishing had no link anywhere in the registry\n                   section, so the publisher half of the story dead-ended. -->\n              <p class=\"sec-actions rv\" style=\"margin-top: 34px\">\n                <a class=\"btn on-clay\" href=\"https://registry.metahub.ai\">Open the registry</a>\n                <a class=\"btn on-clay-ghost\" href=\"https://developer.metahub.ai\"\n                  >Publish to registry</a\n                >\n              </p>\n            </div>\n            <figure class=\"hub-figure rv\" aria-hidden=\"false\">\n              <svg\n                viewBox=\"0 0 360 360\"\n                width=\"100%\"\n                style=\"max-width: 400px\"\n                role=\"img\"\n                aria-label=\"Hub and spoke diagram: one registry serving skills, MCP servers, plugins, and official vendor tools\"\n              >\n                <g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"rgba(250,246,236,0.5)\">\n                  <circle cx=\"180\" cy=\"180\" r=\"128\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 7\" />\n                  <circle cx=\"180\" cy=\"180\" r=\"74\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 6\" />\n                </g>\n                <g stroke=\"rgba(250,246,236,0.65)\" stroke-width=\"1.4\">\n                  <line class=\"sp\" pathLength=\"1\" x1=\"180\" y1=\"180\" x2=\"180\" y2=\"52\" />\n                  <line class=\"sp\" pathLength=\"1\" x1=\"180\" y1=\"180\" x2=\"304\" y2=\"146\" />\n                  <line class=\"sp\" pathLength=\"1\" x1=\"180\" y1=\"180\" x2=\"262\" y2=\"286\" />\n                  <line class=\"sp\" pathLength=\"1\" x1=\"180\" y1=\"180\" x2=\"98\" y2=\"286\" />\n                  <line class=\"sp\" pathLength=\"1\" x1=\"180\" y1=\"180\" x2=\"56\" y2=\"146\" />\n                </g>\n                <circle cx=\"180\" cy=\"180\" r=\"13\" fill=\"#f4eee3\" />\n                <circle cx=\"180\" cy=\"180\" r=\"5.5\" fill=\"#1d1510\" />\n                <g class=\"nds\" fill=\"#f4eee3\">\n                  <circle cx=\"180\" cy=\"52\" r=\"7\" />\n                  <circle cx=\"304\" cy=\"146\" r=\"7\" />\n                  <circle cx=\"262\" cy=\"286\" r=\"7\" />\n                  <circle cx=\"98\" cy=\"286\" r=\"7\" />\n                  <circle cx=\"56\" cy=\"146\" r=\"7\" />\n                </g>\n                <g\n                  class=\"lbs\"\n                  font-family=\"Martian Mono, monospace\"\n                  font-size=\"10.5\"\n                  fill=\"#faf6ec\"\n                  text-anchor=\"middle\"\n                  letter-spacing=\"1\"\n                >\n                  <text x=\"180\" y=\"34\">SKILLS</text>\n                  <text x=\"304\" y=\"128\">MCP SERVERS</text>\n                  <text x=\"262\" y=\"310\">PLUGINS</text>\n                  <text x=\"98\" y=\"310\">AGENTS</text>\n                  <text x=\"56\" y=\"128\">OFFICIAL</text>\n                </g>\n              </svg>\n              <figcaption>one registry \u00b7 every client</figcaption>\n            </figure>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </section>\n\n      <!-- \u2500\u2500 02 evals \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500 -->\n      <section class=\"sec-evals\" id=\"evals\">\n        <div class=\"wrap\">\n          <div class=\"sec-head rv\">\n            <span class=\"sec-num\">02</span>\n            <h2 class=\"display\">Tested, then listed</h2>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"eval-grid\">\n            <div>\n              <p class=\"lede rv\">\n                You cannot tell whether an AI tool works by reading it. Stars measure attention, not\n                behaviour, and a README is a claim its author is grading. So metahub\n                <strong>runs the thing</strong>, with\n                <a href=\"https://github.com/metahub-ai/assay\">Assay</a>, our open-source eval\n                framework: every artifact is executed in a sandbox against real prompts and graded\n                on the transcript, then probed with prompt injection, out-of-scope bait and\n                destructive requests. The score, the failures and the commit it was measured at are\n                published on the listing, whether or not they flatter us.\n              </p>\n              <ul class=\"eval-points rv\">\n                <li>\n                  <span class=\"kicker\">Scored</span>Correctness, instruction-following, safety and\n                  latency, each out of 10, from a real run rather than a checklist someone ticked.\n                </li>\n                <li>\n                  <span class=\"kicker\">Probed</span>Prompt injection, out-of-scope bait, destructive\n                  requests. A tool that complies loses points for it.\n                </li>\n                <li>\n                  <span class=\"kicker\">Pinned</span>Every score names the commit it was measured at,\n                  so what you install is the thing that was graded.\n                </li>\n                <li>\n                  <span class=\"kicker\">Open</span>The rubric, the probes and the judge prompts are\n                  public as <strong>Assay</strong>, Apache-2.0. Run it on your own repo before you\n                  publish, or send a PR when a rubric is wrong.\n                </li>\n              </ul>\n              <p class=\"rv\" style=\"margin-top: 34px\">\n                <a class=\"btn on-ink\" href=\"https://github.com/metahub-ai/assay\"\n                  >Read the code on GitHub \u2197</a\n                >\n              </p>\n            </div>\n            <div\n              class=\"report rv\"\n              role=\"img\"\n              aria-label=\"Example eval report showing structural, documentation, safety, kind-specific, maintenance and behavioral results\"\n            >\n              <div class=\"bar\"><span>eval-report</span><span>harden-task-file @ 8255ee6</span></div>\n              <div class=\"body\">\n                <div class=\"row\">\n                  <span class=\"k\">structural</span><span class=\"pass\">5/5 pass</span>\n                </div>\n                <div class=\"row\">\n                  <span class=\"k\">documentation</span><span class=\"pass\">5/5 pass</span>\n                </div>\n                <div class=\"row\">\n                  <span class=\"k\">safety</span><span class=\"pass\">2/2 pass</span>\n                </div>\n                <div class=\"row\">\n                  <span class=\"k\">kind-specific</span><span class=\"warn\">3/4 warn</span>\n                </div>\n                <div class=\"row\">\n                  <span class=\"cmt\">\u2514 triggers not declared in frontmatter</span><span></span>\n                </div>\n                <div class=\"row\">\n                  <span class=\"k\">maintenance</span><span class=\"warn\">2/3 warn</span>\n                </div>\n                <div class=\"row\">\n                  <span class=\"k\">behavioral</span><span class=\"pass\">ran \u00b7 graded</span>\n                </div>\n                <div class=\"row\">\n                  <span class=\"cmt\">\u2514 driver: consumer model \u00b7 judge: rubric</span><span></span>\n                </div>\n                <div class=\"row\" style=\"margin-top: 12px\">\n                  <span class=\"k\">verdict</span><span class=\"score\">PUBLISH (warn)</span>\n                </div>\n              </div>\n            </div>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </section>\n\n      <!-- \u2500\u2500 03 the road \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500 -->\n      <!-- \u2500\u2500 03 publisher observability \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n           Promoted out of the roadmap list, where it sat as one line tagged\n           \"Live today\" and read like a future promise. It is shipped, it is\n           free, and it is the reason a publisher would choose this registry\n           over dropping a repo on GitHub \u2014 that belongs in a section, not a\n           bullet. Reuses .reg-steps, which is already styled for clay. -->\n      <section class=\"sec-observe\" id=\"observe\">\n        <div class=\"wrap\">\n          <div class=\"sec-head rv\">\n            <span class=\"sec-num\">03</span>\n            <h2 class=\"display\">See it in the wild</h2>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"reg-grid\">\n            <div>\n              <p class=\"lede rv\">\n                Publishing an AI tool usually means never hearing from it again. You get stars,\n                maybe an issue. You never learn whether it actually fired, how slow it was, or which\n                model it ran under.\n                <strong>Every artifact published to metahub gets a free, live dashboard</strong>\n                from its first real invocation. Why? Because you can't improve what you can't\n                measure.\n              </p>\n              <ul class=\"reg-steps rv\">\n                <li>\n                  <span class=\"n\">01</span\n                  ><span\n                    >Wrap the artifact with the open SDK. One line, no config, no separate\n                    account.</span\n                  >\n                </li>\n                <li>\n                  <span class=\"n\">02</span\n                  ><span\n                    >Publish the repo. The dashboard starts filling from the first real invocation,\n                    not from a demo.</span\n                  >\n                </li>\n                <li>\n                  <span class=\"n\">03</span\n                  ><span\n                    >Read invocations, p95 latency, model mix, handoffs and error rate, plus reviews\n                    that only verified installs can leave.</span\n                  >\n                </li>\n              </ul>\n              <!-- One door only. The old secondary (\"Browse what shipped\" \u2192\n                   registry) duplicated section 01's \"Open the registry\"; two\n                   sections selling the same two links read as padding. This\n                   is the publisher beat, so it keeps the publisher door. -->\n              <p class=\"sec-actions rv\" style=\"margin-top: 34px\">\n                <a class=\"btn on-clay\" href=\"https://developer.metahub.ai\">Start publishing</a>\n              </p>\n            </div>\n            <!-- The proof beside the pitch, matching 01's hub diagram and 02's\n                 eval report: the dashboard a publisher actually gets. -->\n            <figure class=\"obs-figure rv\">\n              <div class=\"report obs\">\n                <div class=\"bar\">\n                  <span>observability \u00b7 last 30 days</span>\n                  <span>demo</span>\n                </div>\n                <div class=\"obs-stats\">\n                  <div>\n                    <span class=\"v\">228</span>\n                    <span class=\"l\">invocations</span>\n                    <span class=\"pass\">+18%</span>\n                  </div>\n                  <div>\n                    <span class=\"v\">73ms</span>\n                    <span class=\"l\">p95 latency</span>\n                    <span class=\"pass\">-9%</span>\n                  </div>\n                  <div>\n                    <span class=\"v\">0.1%</span>\n                    <span class=\"l\">error rate</span>\n                    <span class=\"pass\">-0.2pp</span>\n                  </div>\n                </div>\n                <div class=\"body\">\n                  <div class=\"row\">\n                    <span class=\"k\">top models</span><span class=\"cmt\">past 30d</span>\n                  </div>\n                  <div class=\"obs-model\">\n                    <span class=\"k\">claude-sonnet-4-6</span>\n                    <span class=\"obs-track\" aria-hidden=\"true\"\n                      ><span style=\"width: 62%\"></span\n                    ></span>\n                    <span class=\"score\">62%</span>\n                  </div>\n                  <div class=\"obs-model\">\n                    <span class=\"k\">claude-opus-4-7</span>\n                    <span class=\"obs-track\" aria-hidden=\"true\"\n                      ><span style=\"width: 28%\"></span\n                    ></span>\n                    <span class=\"score\">28%</span>\n                  </div>\n                  <div class=\"obs-model\">\n                    <span class=\"k\">ollama/llama3.2</span>\n                    <span class=\"obs-track\" aria-hidden=\"true\"\n                      ><span style=\"width: 10%\"></span\n                    ></span>\n                    <span class=\"score\">10%</span>\n                  </div>\n                  <div class=\"row\" style=\"margin-top: 12px\">\n                    <span class=\"k\">reviews</span><span class=\"pass\">verified installs only</span>\n                  </div>\n                </div>\n              </div>\n            </figure>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </section>\n\n      <section class=\"road\" id=\"road\">\n        <div class=\"wrap\">\n          <div class=\"sec-head rv\">\n            <span class=\"sec-num\">04</span>\n            <h2 class=\"display\">Built in the open</h2>\n          </div>\n          <p class=\"lede rv\" style=\"color: var(--ink-soft); margin-bottom: clamp(28px, 4vw, 48px)\">\n            The registry is the first piece, not the product. The plan is an ecosystem of tools that\n            make AI extensions trustworthy: evaluated on the way in, observable in the wild, and\n            easy to build.\n          </p>\n          <div class=\"road-list rv\">\n            <div class=\"road-item live\">\n              <span class=\"idx\">01</span>\n              <div>\n                <h3>The registry</h3>\n                <p>Catalog, evals, reviews, one-command install into any harness.</p>\n              </div>\n              <span class=\"tag live\">Live today</span>\n            </div>\n            <!-- Order mirrors the page's own narrative and the lede above:\n                 evaluated on the way in (02), observable in the wild (03). -->\n            <div class=\"road-item opening\">\n              <span class=\"idx\">02</span>\n              <div>\n                <h3>The eval framework</h3>\n                <p>\n                  The thinking behind every listing, released as\n                  <a href=\"https://github.com/metahub-ai/assay\">Assay</a>: read the checks, run them\n                  on any repo, and send a PR when a rubric is wrong.\n                </p>\n              </div>\n              <span class=\"tag opening\">Live &amp; open source</span>\n            </div>\n            <div class=\"road-item live\">\n              <span class=\"idx\">03</span>\n              <div>\n                <h3>Publisher observability</h3>\n                <p>Live invocations, latency, model mix and error rate. Free, and shipped.</p>\n              </div>\n              <span class=\"tag live\">Live today</span>\n            </div>\n            <div class=\"road-item\">\n              <span class=\"idx\">04</span>\n              <div>\n                <h3>The wider ecosystem</h3>\n                <p>\n                  More tools are already in build on top of the registry. Each one gets announced\n                  when you can actually use it, not before.\n                </p>\n              </div>\n              <span class=\"tag next\">Next</span>\n            </div>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </section>\n\n      <!-- \u2500\u2500 04 get involved \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500 -->\n      <section id=\"involved\">\n        <div class=\"wrap\">\n          <div class=\"sec-head rv\">\n            <span class=\"sec-num\">05</span>\n            <h2 class=\"display\">Get involved</h2>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"fb-callout rv\">\n            <div>\n              <p class=\"kicker\" style=\"color: var(--clay)\">While you browse</p>\n              <p class=\"fb-line\">\n                Every page of the registry has a <strong>Feedback</strong> button sitting in the\n                corner. Two clicks, no account needed, and the page you were on comes attached. Bug,\n                idea, or just a feeling: we read every single one.\n              </p>\n            </div>\n            <a class=\"btn\" href=\"https://registry.metahub.ai/?feedback=open\">Try it now</a>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"involve-grid\">\n            <div class=\"track clay rv\">\n              <h3>Break it for us</h3>\n              <p>\n                You're early, which means your half-formed complaint is worth more than a thousand\n                stars. Taking 20 minutes to kick the tires is plenty.\n              </p>\n              <ol>\n                <li>\n                  <span\n                    >Browse <code>registry.metahub.ai</code>. Search something you'd actually\n                    use.</span\n                  >\n                </li>\n                <li>\n                  <span\n                    >Install one artifact with <code>mh install</code>, or straight from your AI\n                    client.</span\n                  >\n                </li>\n                <li>\n                  <span\n                    >Leave a review on it. Sign in with GitHub if you want the verified badge.</span\n                  >\n                </li>\n                <li>\n                  <span\n                    >Anything confusing, broken, or slow: hit the <strong>Feedback</strong> button,\n                    bottom-right of every page. It lands on our desk with the page attached.</span\n                  >\n                </li>\n              </ol>\n              <p class=\"cta-row\">\n                <a class=\"btn on-clay\" href=\"https://registry.metahub.ai\">Start browsing</a>\n              </p>\n            </div>\n            <div class=\"track rv\">\n              <h3>Build with us</h3>\n              <p>\n                Publishers and contributors get the ecosystem's best seats: distribution today, a\n                say in what gets built next.\n              </p>\n              <ol>\n                <li>\n                  <span\n                    >Publish your skill, MCP server, or plugin at <code>developer.metahub.ai</code>.\n                    GitHub repo in, live listing out, in about 90 seconds.</span\n                  >\n                </li>\n                <li>\n                  <span\n                    >Get free observability the day you publish: invocations, latency,\n                    reviews.</span\n                  >\n                </li>\n                <li>\n                  <span\n                    >Watch the eval framework land in the open. Early testers hear about it\n                    first.</span\n                  >\n                </li>\n              </ol>\n              <p class=\"cta-row\">\n                <a class=\"btn\" href=\"https://developer.metahub.ai\">Publish a tool</a>\n              </p>\n            </div>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </section>\n\n      <!-- \u2500\u2500 06 questions \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500 -->\n      <!-- Four questions the homepage already answers in prose, restated in\n           the question form an assistant actually matches against. They are\n           deliberately the PRACTICAL set (install, editors, telemetry, the\n           listing bar); /faq keeps the identity set (what metahub is, what it\n           makes, what it costs) and the registry FAQ keeps installing and\n           publishing. That is the same split-by-subject rule the two FAQs\n           already follow, extended to a third surface rather than broken.\n           Answers stay visible and un-collapsed: text a crawler must expand\n           is text it may not count, and hidden answers void FAQPage markup. -->\n      <section id=\"questions\">\n        <div class=\"wrap\">\n          <div class=\"sec-head rv\">\n            <span class=\"sec-num\">06</span>\n            <h2 class=\"display\">Common questions</h2>\n          </div>\n          <p class=\"lede rv\">\n            The four we get asked before anyone runs the install command. What metahub is, what it\n            makes, and what it costs are answered on the <a href=\"/faq\">full FAQ</a>; installing\n            from the catalog and publishing into it are answered in the\n            <a href=\"https://registry.metahub.ai/faq\">registry FAQ</a>.\n          </p>\n          <div class=\"faq-grid\">\n            <div class=\"faq-item rv\">\n              <h3>How do I install metahub?</h3>\n              <p>One command, and a plain shell script you can read before you run it:</p>\n              <!-- Its own line, like the hero's install block. Inline, the chip's\n                   padding butted straight into the following period and the URL\n                   broke across two lines mid-path. -->\n              <p><code class=\"faq-cmd\">curl -fsSL https://metahub.ai/install.sh | sh</code></p>\n              <p>\n                It installs the mh CLI and the metahub MCP server through your package manager, adds\n                them to PATH, and connects the MCP server to the AI editors it finds on your\n                machine, a step you can skip and reverse later. After that a single mh install\n                command works in any supported editor.\n              </p>\n            </div>\n            <div class=\"faq-item rv\">\n              <h3>Which AI clients does metahub work with?</h3>\n              <p>\n                Eleven, including Claude Code, Cursor, and Zed. The same install command wires an\n                artifact into whichever of them you use, so a skill, MCP server, agent, or plugin\n                does not need separate setup instructions per editor.\n              </p>\n            </div>\n            <div class=\"faq-item rv\">\n              <h3>What does metahub send back when I install a tool?</h3>\n              <p>\n                Anonymous, opt-out usage statistics, and nothing else. Installed tools report\n                invocation counts, latency, and error rates back to the person who published them.\n                Never your prompts, never your files. You can switch the telemetry off.\n              </p>\n            </div>\n            <div class=\"faq-item rv\">\n              <h3>How does metahub decide what gets listed?</h3>\n              <p>\n                Every artifact is evaluated before it reaches the catalog. The evaluation runs on\n                <a href=\"https://github.com/metahub-ai/assay\">Assay</a>, our open-source framework\n                released under Apache-2.0, so both the checks and the scoring are public. You can\n                run the same evaluation on your own repository, and open a pull request when you\n                think a check is wrong.\n              </p>\n            </div>\n          </div>\n          <p class=\"lede rv\" style=\"margin-top: 28px\">\n            Still stuck, or want to know who is building this?\n            <a href=\"/contact\">Get in touch</a> or read <a href=\"/about\">what metahub is for</a>.\n          </p>\n        </div>\n      </section>\n    </main>\n\n    <footer>\n      <div class=\"wrap\">\n        <div class=\"foot-grid\">\n          <p class=\"foot-big rv\">Tools,<br /><span>trusted.</span></p>\n          <nav class=\"foot-links\" aria-label=\"Footer\">\n            <a href=\"https://registry.metahub.ai\">/registry</a>\n            <a href=\"https://developer.metahub.ai\">/developer</a>\n            <a href=\"#evals\">/eval</a>\n            <a href=\"/faq\">faq</a>\n            <a href=\"/about\">about</a>\n            <a href=\"/contact\">contact</a>\n            <a href=\"#involved\">get involved</a>\n          </nav>\n        </div>\n        <div class=\"foot-meta\">\n          <span>metahub \u00b7 built in the open</span>\n          <span>\u00a9 2026 metahub</span>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </footer>\n  </body>\n</html>\n", "erro": "", "ms": 1118}